    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d104">

                <pb facs="0244" n="132" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg132"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.00/5893: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Denmark</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Schoenfeld</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Copenhagen</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-12-13T14:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 13,
                                1919, 2 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 7:11
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>307. Today’s newspapers publish semiofficial announcement reporting return by
                    British, French, Italian, American Legations of Litvinoff’s peace note. Announcement adds that inasmuch as
                        Litvinoff came with British
                    safe-conduct for humanitarian and non-political reasons and Denmark consented to
                    his coming on that understanding only, it would have been breach of faith toward
                        <pb facs="0245" n="133" xml:id="pg133"/> Denmark to permit his sojourn here
                    to be used for political purposes. Announcement concludes by stating that this
                    diplomatic action merely means that Allied Powers have taken no official
                    cognizance of Litvinoff’s <hi
                        rendition="#italic">démarche</hi> and that their action is in conformity
                    with Great Britain’s engagements toward Denmark.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Schoenfeld</hi></signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>[For further information concerning the Soviet régime and the attitude of the
                        Department toward it, see “Memorandum on Certain Aspects of the Bolshevik
                        Movement in Russia”, Washington, Government Printing Office, 1919. This
                        memorandum was transmitted by the Secretary of State to the Chairman of the
                        Senate Committee on Foreign Relations under cover of a letter dated October
                        27, 1919, and reprinted as Senate Document No. 172, 66th Congress, 2d
                        session.]</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
